<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        Each person files their own return, but a household often shares accounts and pools claims. The
        <strong>Household</strong> section at the foot of the <a href="tax">Tax report</a> records who's who, whose claims and
        income are whose, and gives each person a rough estimate of their tax.
    </p>

    <h3>People</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <span class="help-ui">Household: who claims what, where they live, whose income</span> at the bottom of the Tax report.</li>
        <li>Type a name and choose <span class="help-ui">Add person</span> for each person who files. Rename or remove them later.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Once there's anyone in the household:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Each claim gets a <span class="help-ui">Claimed by</span> picker, for example child care to the lower-income spouse, or pooled
            medical expenses and donations to one person.</li>
        <li>The picker beside the tax year shows <em>Everyone</em>, one person, or the claims <em>Not assigned</em> yet. The CSV and the
            printable summary follow it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Each person's situation</h3>
    <p>Saved as you change it, per person and per year:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Province on Dec 31</strong>: sets the provincial rates. No estimate without it.</li>
        <li><strong>Employer withholds tax from pay</strong>: untick for income with no tax taken off, so it isn't counted as already paid.</li>
        <li><strong>Pension adjustment</strong> and <strong>RRSP deduction limit</strong>: from your T4 and last year's notice of assessment.
            Leave them blank to estimate from this year's pay.</li>
        <li><strong>Share of joint rental</strong>: for property owned with others, this person's share of the rent and of the rental
            expenses no one else claimed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Whose income</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Whose accounts</strong>: income deposited into someone's own account counts as theirs; mark joint accounts
            <em>Shared</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Income in shared accounts</strong>: for deposits into a shared account, choose whose each payer's deposits are (an
            employer's pay to one spouse, say). Rent from property you own together can be <em>split between co-owners</em> by their shares.
            A deposit from a new payer still needs choosing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The tax estimate</h3>
    <p>
        At the top of the report, LoonieBooks works out tax on the Income › Salary, Business Income and Rental Income deposits in
        the ledger, takes off the deductions and credits confirmed on the report, and shows roughly what's left to pay or get
        back: the <strong>Estimated refund</strong> or <strong>balance owing</strong>, what's <strong>Already paid</strong> through
        withholding, the <strong>Marginal rate</strong> on your next dollar, and how much <strong>RRSP room</strong> is left and what using
        it would save. <span class="help-ui">How it's worked out</span> shows the full calculation.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>With several people, choose one in the picker at the top for their full calculation.</li>
        <li>Rents are projected to December 31 from the deposits so far; expenses only count once they're tagged.</li>
        <li>Only years LoonieBooks has federal and provincial rates for can be estimated; the card says so otherwise.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="help-caution">
        The estimate is a rough guide for planning, built only from what's in your ledger. It isn't your return or tax advice;
        slips, other income and credits it doesn't know about all change the real figure.
    </p>
</div>
